# What a loop that hashes its own answer holds, watched (kanso#2299).
#
# `looped` hands the digest of `u` to its next iteration and carries a list it
# never reads. Each digest is thirty-two bytes, and everything `sha256/digest`
# built to make one is dead when the next iteration starts, so the arena
# should hold one iteration's work however many there are.
#
# `sha256/digest` answered through `list/fold`, which every caller in a
# program shares, so the compiler read its answer as any value at all, and a
# slot that may hold any value is never carried across a rewind.
#
# Watched, not frozen: regenerate deliberately and say which way it went.
import "std/sha256"

pub fn carried u left
  looped [1 2 3] u left (left == 0)

fn looped _ u _ true
  u

fn looped p u left false
  next = sha256/digest u
  looped p next (left - 1) (left - 1 == 0)

pub play = print (carried [1 2 3 4] 600)
